{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 12, :comments ["<b>And they were loathsome because of the children of Israel.</b> Rashi explained: they were like thorns in their eyes, meaning that Israel was compared in their eyes to a painful thorn and a burning thorn-bush. But the language does not bear this out, for it says ״מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ and how could Israel be like thorns מִפְּנֵי the children of Israel? Moreover, ״וַיָּקֻצוּ״ is in the nifʿal (passive) form with a shureq, and from this it appears that it refers to the Egyptians and not to Israel. And if he meant that it was as though thorns had entered their bodies, he should have said ״וַיָּקֻצוּ מִן בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל.״ Therefore it appears to me that the Egyptians likened themselves in their own eyes to thorns, for it is the way of the world that one uproots thorns from the field in order to plant in their place a choice plant, as it is written (Psalms 80:9–10): ״You plucked up a vine from Egypt; You drove out nations and planted it. You cleared a path before it and rooted its roots,״ etc. The Holy One, blessed be He, wanted to plant in the land of Israel a choice plant — that is, Israel, of whom it says ״A vineyard of foaming wine״ (Isaiah 27:2) — and to uproot from there the nations, which were likened to thorns. So too the Egyptians thought, after they saw the will of the Blessed El that the people of Israel multiplied and increased exceedingly, that it was the will of the El that Israel be planted in the land of Egypt while they would be like thorns cast out of the earth, as it is written ״and she shall rise from upon the earth״ (Hosea 2:1) — whereas it should have said ״and we shall rise,״ were it not that He attached their curse to others. And this is what is written: ״וַיָּקֻצוּ מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל״ — that the Egyptians likened themselves in their own eyes to thorns cast out of the earth, מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל, who would be planted in their place." "Another matter: ״וַיָּקֻצוּ מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל,״ as is deduced in Shemot Rabbah on this parashah (2:5): Why did the Holy One, blessed be He, reveal Himself to Moses in the thorn-bush? Rabbi Yosi says: Just as the thorn-bush is harsher than all the trees, and any bird that enters it does not come out in peace, etc. Therefore it says ״וַיָּקֻצוּ,״ that the Egyptians were likened to these thorns, for when a bird enters into them it enters easily, but when it wants to come out it cannot, because of the stabbing points of the thorns. So too the Egyptians brought them into servitude lightly, with soft speech, and afterward בְּפָרֶךְ (with rigor), for they did not let them go out from the servitude. Therefore Scripture juxtaposed ״וַיָּקֻצוּ״ to ״בְּפָרֶךְ״ mentioned in the parashah, for they are entirely of the same subject as ״וַיָּקֻצוּ,״ that the Egyptians were likened to thorny bushes because of what they did to the children of Israel. Therefore it says ״מִפְּנֵי בְּנֵי יִשְׂרָאֵל.״"], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "The verse citation הושע ב א follows the Hebrew verse numbering; in English Bibles this is Hosea 2:3.", 1 "The rabbinic source is cited as רבתי; this refers to שמות רבה (Shemot Rabbah)."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:17:50.363538112Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}